        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in District 29: A Comprehensive Guide</h1>

  <p>In District 29 (Cambria Heights, Laurelton, Queens Village, Springfield Gardens, Rosedale), protecting student rights is paramount. This guide provides information and actionable advice to help parents navigate disciplinary actions, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights for students, particularly those with IEPs or 504 plans. Ensuring a safe and supportive learning environment requires clear disciplinary procedures that respect these rights, and this guide aims to empower you with the knowledge you need.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Student Discipline in NYC & District 29</h2>

  <p>The New York City Department of Education (NYC DOE) has specific regulations regarding student discipline. It is crucial to understand these rules to advocate effectively for your child. The <a href="https://www.schools.nyc.gov/school-life/rules-for-students/discipline-code" target="_blank">NYC DOE's Citywide Behavioral Expectations to Support Student Learning</a> outlines these expectations and disciplinary procedures. District 29 schools are required to adhere to these citywide policies.</p>

  <p>Disciplinary actions can range from verbal warnings and detentions to suspensions and, in rare cases, expulsion. The severity of the consequence should be proportionate to the offense. However, for students with disabilities, additional safeguards are in place to ensure fairness and prevent discriminatory discipline.</p>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): Protecting Students with IEPs and 504 Plans</h2>

  <p>A crucial aspect of student discipline, especially for students with Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) or 504 plans, is the Manifestation Determination Review (MDR). This review is triggered when a student faces disciplinary action that constitutes a "change in placement." According to the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), a change in placement occurs if a student is:
  <ul>
      <li>Removed from their current placement for more than 10 consecutive school days; or</li>
      <li>Subjected to a series of removals that constitute a pattern because they total more than 10 school days in a school year; and</li>
      <li>The student's behavior is substantially similar to the student's behavior in previous incidents that resulted in the series of removals; and</li>
      <li>Additional factors such as the length of each removal, the total amount of time the student is removed, and the proximity of the removals to one another.</li>
  </ul>

  The MDR team, including parents/guardians, school administrators, special education teachers, general education teachers (if the student is in general education), and relevant IEP team members, convene to determine if the student's behavior was a manifestation of their disability. The parent should be notified of the MDR meeting and provided with all relevant documentation prior to the meeting. The parent is a critical member of the MDR team. </p>

  <p><b>The Key Questions of an MDR (as per IDEA):</b>
  <ol>
    <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
    <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
  </ol>
  </p>

  <p><b>If the Behavior Is a Manifestation:</b></p>

  <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the student's disability (either because of a link to the disability itself, or due to the district's failure to implement the IEP correctly), the school must take immediate corrective action. According to 34 CFR § 300.530(f):
    <ul>
      <li>The school must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) for the child; or</li>
      <li>If a BIP already exists, the IEP team must review and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior.</li>
    </ul>
   Furthermore, unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP modification, the student must return to their current educational placement. Disciplinary action may not be applied in the same way to students whose behavior is a manifestation of their disability.</p>

   <p><b>If the Behavior Is NOT a Manifestation:</b></p>

   <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the student's disability, the student may be subject to the same disciplinary actions as students without disabilities. However, the school district must still provide FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education), as mandated by IDEA.</p>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Prior to the MDR meeting, thoroughly review your child's IEP, any relevant behavioral data, and any incident reports related to the disciplinary action. Prepare a written statement outlining your perspective on the incident and how it relates (or doesn't relate) to your child's disability. Bring any supporting documentation to the meeting. Don't hesitate to request an adjournment of the MDR meeting if you need more time to prepare or consult with an advocate or attorney.</p>

  <h2>Suspensions in District 29: Your Rights and Responsibilities</h2>

  <p>Suspensions can be either in-school or out-of-school. New York State Education Law § 3214 outlines the procedures for suspensions. Short-term suspensions (5 days or less) typically require verbal or written notification to parents. Long-term suspensions (more than 5 days) require a formal hearing where the student has the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and be represented by counsel.</p>

  <p><b>Advice Regarding Suspensions in District 29:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Know Your Rights:</b> Review the NYC Department of Education's Student Discipline Code, particularly Section 3214 of the Education Law and Part 100 of the Commissioner's Regulations.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep records of all communication, incidents, and disciplinary actions. Note the date, time, and names of everyone involved. Save emails, letters, and any other relevant documents.</li>
    <li><b>Attend All Meetings:</b> Participate actively in any hearings or meetings related to the suspension. Ask questions, present your perspective, and ensure your child's voice is heard.</li>
    <li><b>Consider Legal Counsel:</b> If you believe your child's rights have been violated, or if the suspension is long-term, consult with an education attorney or advocate experienced in New York special education law.</li>
    <li><b>Request Records:</b> You have the right to access your child's educational records, including disciplinary records. Request these records to understand the basis for the suspension.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It is critical that parents are kept informed and given an opportunity to share their perspective throughout the disciplinary process. The school must provide timely and adequate notice of any disciplinary action. According to Part 200.2(b)(1)(ix) of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education, parents have the right to participate in meetings regarding their child's identification, evaluation, educational placement, and the provision of a free appropriate public education (FAPE). This includes disciplinary meetings.</p>

  <h2>Due Process Rights for Students with Disabilities in District 29</h2>

  <p>If you disagree with a school's disciplinary decision, including a manifestation determination or a suspension, you have the right to pursue due process. This involves filing a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and requesting a hearing before an Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO). The due process complaint must be filed within two years of the date you knew or should have known about the actions that form the basis of the complaint.
  </p>
  <p><b>Steps to File a Due Process Complaint:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Gather Information:</b> Collect all relevant documentation, including IEPs, evaluations, incident reports, suspension notices, and any other communication with the school.</li>
    <li><b>Consult with an Expert:</b> Speak with an education attorney or advocate to discuss your options and develop a strategy.</li>
    <li><b>Draft the Complaint:</b> The due process complaint must include specific information, such as the child's name, address, school, and a description of the issues in dispute. It must also propose a resolution to the problem.</li>
    <li><b>File the Complaint:</b> File the complaint with NYSED. The complaint can be found on the NYSED website.</li>
    <li><b>Resolution Meeting:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution meeting with the parents to attempt to resolve the dispute.</li>
    <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> If the resolution meeting is unsuccessful, an impartial hearing will be conducted. You will have the opportunity to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine the school district's witnesses.</li>
    <li><b>Hearing Officer Decision:</b> The IHO will issue a written decision within 45 days of the resolution period.</li>
    <li><b>Appeals:</b> If you disagree with the IHO's decision, you can appeal to the State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Failing to Document:</b> Keep meticulous records of all interactions with the school.</li>
      <li><b>Missing Deadlines:</b> Be aware of deadlines for filing complaints and requesting hearings. Missing a deadline can jeopardize your case.</li>
      <li><b>Going It Alone:</b> Don't hesitate to seek professional help from an education attorney or advocate. Navigating the special education system can be complex and overwhelming.</li>
      <li><b>Not Understanding the IEP:</b> Thoroughly review your child's IEP to ensure it is being properly implemented. A failure to implement the IEP can be a strong argument in a manifestation determination or due process hearing.</li>
      <li><b>Accepting "No" for an Answer:</b> Be persistent in advocating for your child's rights. If you believe the school is not providing FAPE, don't give up.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>FAQ: Discipline & Special Education in District 29</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What is a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA)?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> An FBA is a process used to identify the purpose or function of a student's behavior. It involves collecting data on the behavior, identifying the triggers and consequences, and developing a hypothesis about why the behavior is occurring. The information gathered from the FBA is used to develop a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What is a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP)?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> A BIP is a written plan that outlines strategies to address a student's challenging behavior. It includes proactive strategies to prevent the behavior from occurring, as well as reactive strategies to manage the behavior when it does occur. The BIP should be based on the results of an FBA and should be individualized to meet the student's specific needs.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if my child's behavior is caused by bullying?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Bullying is never acceptable. If your child's behavior is related to bullying, the school must take immediate action to address the bullying and protect your child. The IEP team should also consider whether the bullying is impacting your child's disability and whether the IEP needs to be modified to address the bullying.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior that is related to their disability?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Generally, no. If a student's behavior is determined to be a manifestation of their disability, they cannot be suspended in the same way as students without disabilities. The school must focus on addressing the underlying cause of the behavior and providing appropriate supports and services.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What is an Interim Alternative Educational Setting (IAES)?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> An IAES is a temporary placement for a student who has been suspended or removed from their current placement for disciplinary reasons. The IAES must provide the student with the opportunity to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in another setting, and to continue to receive the services and supports outlined in their IEP.</dd>

	<dt><b>Q: Does the school district have to notify ACS?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> If the school district suspects educational neglect, they are required to report this to the Administration for Children's Services (ACS). However, the school district should be very cautious about equating a disability-related behavior to educational neglect. Parents should be consulted before making a report.</dd>
    </dl>

</body>
